# Target C — Type-II loaded unit contacts are force-hull sides

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module centralizes a structural theorem that had been duplicated across several
post-123 research branches.

Let `F_D=conv(B_D)` be the loaded force hull in the canonical Type-II branch. Then for
all sufficiently large Type-II survivors,

\[
\boxed{
 b,b'\in B_D,\ |b-b'|=1
 \Longrightarrow
 b,b'\text{ are consecutive vertices of }F_D.
}
\tag{LH.1}
\]

Equivalently, in canonical Section-63 notation,

\[
\boxed{c_B=0.}
\tag{LH.2}
\]

Thus every loaded-loaded unit contact is a unit side of the global loaded hull.

## 1. Finite near-disk chord lemma

Let `K` be a convex polygon satisfying

\[
d_H(K,B(o,R))\le\delta<R.
\]

Translate `o` to the origin and put

\[
r:=R-\delta,
\qquad R_+:=R+\delta.
\]

For convex compact sets, Hausdorff distance is uniform support-function distance, so

\[
B(0,r)\subseteq K\subseteq B(0,R_+).
\tag{LH.3}
\]

If `x in partial K` and `u` is any outward unit supporting normal at `x`, then

\[
u\cdot x=h_K(u)\ge r,
\qquad |x|\le R_+.
\]

Hence

\[
\angle(u,x)\le
\beta:=\arccos(r/R_+).
\tag{LH.4}
\]

Now let `p,q` be vertices with `|p-q|=1`. Since `|p|,|q|>=r`, if `gamma` is their
smaller radial angle then

\[
1=|p-q|\ge2r\sin(\gamma/2),
\]

so

\[
\gamma\le2\arcsin(1/(2r)).
\tag{LH.5}
\]

Because the origin is interior to `K`, radial projection preserves cyclic order on
`partial K`. Choose the boundary arc from `p` to `q` through the shorter radial
interval. Every side on this arc has outward normal within `beta` of a radial
direction in that interval. Consequently all consistently oriented side directions on
this arc lie in one angular interval of width at most

\[
\tau:=2\arccos(r/R_+)+2\arcsin(1/(2r)).
\tag{LH.6}
\]

Assume every side of `K` has length at least one and `tau<2pi/3`. If `p,q` were
nonconsecutive, the chosen arc would contain at least two oriented side vectors
`v_1,...,v_j`, `j>=2`. Let `w` be the midpoint direction of their common angular
interval. Then

\[
\begin{aligned}
1=|q-p|
&\ge w\cdot(q-p)\\
&=\sum_iw\cdot v_i\\
&\ge\sum_i|v_i|\cos(\tau/2)\\
&\ge2\cos(\tau/2)>1,
\end{aligned}
\]

which is impossible. Therefore every unit chord is a polygon side.

## 2. Type-II application

Canonical Section 80 gives centers `o_D` with

\[
\delta_D:=d_H(F_D,B(o_D,D/2))=o(D).
\]

Set

\[
r_D=D/2-\delta_D,
\qquad R_{+,D}=D/2+\delta_D.
\]

Then

\[
r_D/R_{+,D}\to1,
\qquad r_D\to\infty,
\]

so the width `tau_D` from (LH.6) tends to zero. Every force-hull side joins two source
points and therefore has length at least one. The finite lemma applies for all
sufficiently large `D`, proving (LH.1).

Canonical Section 63 defines `c_B` as the loaded-loaded unit contacts beyond the unit
hull sides. Hence (LH.1) gives (LH.2), and

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}=q-m,}
\tag{LH.7}
\]

where `m` is the number of nonunit loaded-hull sides.

The Section-63 ledgers consequently simplify to

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B,}
\tag{LH.8}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{c_I+s_C=m+k-2.}
\tag{LH.9}
\]

## 3. Hostile-audit checks

The exact conclusion is stronger than the earlier `c_B=o(D)` result, so the following
points were rechecked independently.

- The proof requires only `delta_D/D->0`; the absolute Hausdorff error may diverge.
- From `B(0,r) subset K`, every boundary point has radial norm at least `r`.
- For fixed radial angle, two radii at least `r` have mutual distance at least
  `2r sin(gamma/2)`, so (LH.5) has the correct direction.
- The short **polar** boundary arc is used, not the complementary Gauss-map arc.
- At any relative-interior point of a hull side, its outward normal obeys (LH.4), so
  the side-direction cone bound is valid on the whole selected arc.
- Consistent boundary orientation converts the normal cone into one tangent-direction
  interval of the same width.
- If a unit chord were nonconsecutive, the selected arc contains at least two hull
  sides, each of length at least one. Since `tau/2<pi/3`, their projection sum is
  strictly greater than one.
- Unit hull sides remain allowed; only nonconsecutive unit chords are excluded.
- Contact-isolated source points are untouched and no full-source affine-kernel claim
  is used.

## 4. Scope

This theorem is Type II only because it uses the near-disk force-hull convergence. It
does not control nonunit hull sides, the inactive-container branch, or the aggregate
outer-detour currency by itself.

**Verdict:** PASS at audited-working scope. C-II remains open.